WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.259 --> 00:00:01.576
- Good morning Believe Nation.

00:00:01.576 --> 00:00:04.760
Today's message is dare to fail.

00:00:04.760 --> 00:00:06.692
Over to you, Aaron Sorkin.

00:00:06.692 --> 00:00:09.005
(rooster crows)

00:00:09.005 --> 00:00:11.439
♫ I wave up every morning

00:00:11.439 --> 00:00:14.276
♫ Espresso keep me going

00:00:14.276 --> 00:00:18.302
♫ I wake up every morning

00:00:18.302 --> 00:00:20.831
- You'll meet a lot of
people who, to put it simply,

00:00:20.831 --> 00:00:23.477
don't know what they're talking about.

00:00:23.477 --> 00:00:26.539
In 1970, a CBS executive famously said

00:00:26.539 --> 00:00:29.912
that there were four things
we would never, ever see

00:00:29.912 --> 00:00:33.963
on television, a divorced
person, a Jewish person,

00:00:33.963 --> 00:00:35.665
a person living in New York City,

00:00:35.665 --> 00:00:37.832
and a man with a mustache.

00:00:38.987 --> 00:00:42.538
By 1980, every show on television
was about a divorced Jew

00:00:42.538 --> 00:00:44.485
who lives in New York City
and goes on a blind date

00:00:44.485 --> 00:00:45.494
with Tom Selleck.

00:00:45.494 --> 00:00:48.327
(audience laughs)

00:00:49.410 --> 00:00:52.204
Develop your own compass and trust it.

00:00:52.204 --> 00:00:53.196
Take risks.

00:00:53.196 --> 00:00:54.506
Dare to fail.

00:00:54.506 --> 00:00:56.728
Remember, the first
person through the wall

00:00:56.728 --> 00:00:58.451
always gets hurt.

00:00:58.451 --> 00:01:00.860
You're going to fall down,
but the world doesn't care

00:01:00.860 --> 00:01:03.930
how many times you fall down
as long as it's one fewer

00:01:03.930 --> 00:01:06.808
than the number of times you get back up.

00:01:06.808 --> 00:01:09.201
- So failure is one of
the most common themes

00:01:09.201 --> 00:01:12.806
that I come across on the
channel, and if you look at,

00:01:12.806 --> 00:01:15.551
one side, the people who
are so afraid to fail,

00:01:15.551 --> 00:01:19.482
and then on the second, the
people who are successful

00:01:19.482 --> 00:01:21.971
have failed so many times.

00:01:21.971 --> 00:01:24.556
Any successful person that you look up to,

00:01:24.556 --> 00:01:26.225
anybody in any field,

00:01:26.225 --> 00:01:29.479
got there by failing and
failing and failing and failing.

00:01:29.479 --> 00:01:32.679
They've had a few successes
and way more failures.

00:01:32.679 --> 00:01:34.665
There's a couple things around
failure that I want to address

00:01:34.665 --> 00:01:36.022
thanks to this video.

00:01:36.022 --> 00:01:39.297
The first is, the message of
this video is dare to fail.

00:01:39.297 --> 00:01:41.987
I don't think you have to
be that daring to fail.

00:01:41.987 --> 00:01:46.206
I don't think you have to go
out and do some huge, big thing

00:01:46.206 --> 00:01:49.778
and mortgage your house
and go all in on this idea

00:01:49.778 --> 00:01:52.703
and it has to work and
it's this massive project.

00:01:52.703 --> 00:01:53.929
That's one option.

00:01:53.929 --> 00:01:57.316
I think you can fail way
smaller, and I think you should.

00:01:57.316 --> 00:01:59.496
I think as soon as you get an idea

00:01:59.496 --> 00:02:01.011
that you want to try something,

00:02:01.011 --> 00:02:04.294
that you owe it to
yourself to go out and try,

00:02:04.294 --> 00:02:08.324
and fail as small as possible just to see.

00:02:08.324 --> 00:02:10.467
Like, you want to start a YouTube channel.

00:02:10.467 --> 00:02:12.275
Maybe that's one of your
goals or aspirations.

00:02:12.275 --> 00:02:13.964
"I want to start a YouTube channel."

00:02:13.964 --> 00:02:14.922
Great.

00:02:14.922 --> 00:02:16.975
What's involved in
starting a YouTube channel?

00:02:16.975 --> 00:02:17.925
And suddenly, you're going to say,

00:02:17.925 --> 00:02:19.530
"Well, I need a camera and I need lighting

00:02:19.530 --> 00:02:21.857
"and I need a microphone
and I need to do research.

00:02:21.857 --> 00:02:23.065
"I need to learn editing skills

00:02:23.065 --> 00:02:24.029
"or I need to hire an editor."

00:02:24.029 --> 00:02:25.642
And you start adding
up all of these things,

00:02:25.642 --> 00:02:29.517
and all they're doing is putting
road blocks in front of you

00:02:29.517 --> 00:02:31.739
that prevent you from doing anything.

00:02:31.739 --> 00:02:35.736
And instead of going big
with it, I'd say go small.

00:02:35.736 --> 00:02:36.714
Just start.

00:02:36.714 --> 00:02:39.732
Because so many people
will start it, will try,

00:02:39.732 --> 00:02:41.947
like you spent all this
money buying all the gear,

00:02:41.947 --> 00:02:44.210
doing all this stuff,
and then you do a couple,

00:02:44.210 --> 00:02:45.521
and you don't like it.

00:02:45.521 --> 00:02:47.220
How many things have you gone and tried

00:02:47.220 --> 00:02:48.780
and you didn't enjoy it?

00:02:48.780 --> 00:02:50.052
Or maybe you enjoyed it once,

00:02:50.052 --> 00:02:51.286
but it's not going to be the thing

00:02:51.286 --> 00:02:52.810
that you want to do for
the rest of your life

00:02:52.810 --> 00:02:54.689
or at least the next couple years?

00:02:54.689 --> 00:02:56.513
And that happens to so many people!

00:02:56.513 --> 00:02:59.262
"I'm going to invest all
this money into all this gear

00:02:59.262 --> 00:03:03.914
"to make great videos," and
then you make two, and you stop,

00:03:03.914 --> 00:03:05.723
because it's not what you want to do,

00:03:05.723 --> 00:03:07.751
and you're stuck with all this gear.

00:03:07.751 --> 00:03:09.013
And so when you have this idea

00:03:09.013 --> 00:03:12.974
of something that you
want to do, just start.

00:03:12.974 --> 00:03:16.419
Start, with no money, with no connections,

00:03:16.419 --> 00:03:17.723
with no resources.

00:03:17.723 --> 00:03:19.032
Just start.

00:03:19.032 --> 00:03:21.751
If you want to start a YouTube channel,

00:03:21.751 --> 00:03:24.447
then you know that you
have a phone to do it,

00:03:24.447 --> 00:03:25.961
and if you don't have a phone,

00:03:25.961 --> 00:03:27.735
then one of your friends has a phone,

00:03:27.735 --> 00:03:30.818
and you can borrow it from
them and make your first video.

00:03:30.818 --> 00:03:32.721
The video quality is good enough.

00:03:32.721 --> 00:03:34.320
The audio quality is good enough.

00:03:34.320 --> 00:03:37.153
You can go outside and
get some decent light,

00:03:37.153 --> 00:03:38.888
and you are good.

00:03:38.888 --> 00:03:41.507
Make that video and start.

00:03:41.507 --> 00:03:44.288
And if it works and you love
it and you want to eat it up,

00:03:44.288 --> 00:03:45.744
you want to do more, great.

00:03:45.744 --> 00:03:46.939
Make another one, and make another one,

00:03:46.939 --> 00:03:48.019
and make another one.

00:03:48.019 --> 00:03:49.324
You can make a business case around it?

00:03:49.324 --> 00:03:50.157
Amazing.

00:03:50.157 --> 00:03:51.188
Buy a proper camera.

00:03:51.188 --> 00:03:52.204
Hire an editor.

00:03:52.204 --> 00:03:53.037
Get lighting.

00:03:53.037 --> 00:03:53.964
Get a microphone.

00:03:53.964 --> 00:03:55.005
Work your way up.

00:03:55.005 --> 00:03:57.157
Or, you do the first one, and you hate it,

00:03:57.157 --> 00:03:58.749
and you realize you never
want to do it again.

00:03:58.749 --> 00:03:59.742
Perfect!

00:03:59.742 --> 00:04:02.239
Get the knowledge, because otherwise,

00:04:02.239 --> 00:04:04.484
you have all these dreams
that just sit in your head,

00:04:04.484 --> 00:04:05.442
"I wish I could do this.

00:04:05.442 --> 00:04:06.790
"I wish I could do this."

00:04:06.790 --> 00:04:08.454
All these things just sit up as wishes

00:04:08.454 --> 00:04:10.782
that never result in any thing.

00:04:10.782 --> 00:04:14.454
Go out and do, and fail on a small scale.

00:04:14.454 --> 00:04:18.515
It does not have to be some
big, bold, daring dream or goal,

00:04:18.515 --> 00:04:21.951
and even if it is, chunk it
down to what I can do today

00:04:21.951 --> 00:04:23.461
to get started on that.

00:04:23.461 --> 00:04:26.460
Whatever huge, big dream,
ambitious goal, you have,

00:04:26.460 --> 00:04:28.335
amazing, I love it.

00:04:28.335 --> 00:04:30.551
What's the first small
step that you can do

00:04:30.551 --> 00:04:32.530
to take action towards it?

00:04:32.530 --> 00:04:35.832
The other thing about failure
that I need to address

00:04:35.832 --> 00:04:38.044
is people see it as some final thing,

00:04:38.044 --> 00:04:39.699
that because you failed,

00:04:39.699 --> 00:04:42.510
because the first thing didn't
work out, then that's it.

00:04:42.510 --> 00:04:44.684
Failure is not a bad thing.

00:04:44.684 --> 00:04:47.741
Failure is just feedback
on how not to do something.

00:04:47.741 --> 00:04:49.224
Failure just means that you can get up

00:04:49.224 --> 00:04:51.024
and do something better the next time.

00:04:51.024 --> 00:04:52.524
If I go back on my YouTube channel

00:04:52.524 --> 00:04:55.724
and look at my first video,
it wasn't very good, right?

00:04:55.724 --> 00:04:57.965
It's not something that
I am insanely proud of

00:04:57.965 --> 00:05:00.753
apart from having the courage,
I guess, to go out and do it,

00:05:00.753 --> 00:05:03.054
but it's not something that
I watch over and over again,

00:05:03.054 --> 00:05:05.316
and like, "Man, look
at this amazing video."

00:05:05.316 --> 00:05:07.443
The video was not very good.

00:05:07.443 --> 00:05:09.464
But it also wasn't a failure.

00:05:09.464 --> 00:05:12.643
Just because it didn't get
100,000 or 1,000,000 views,

00:05:12.643 --> 00:05:13.688
it wasn't a failure.

00:05:13.688 --> 00:05:16.965
It was the best effort that I
could've done at that point.

00:05:16.965 --> 00:05:18.960
And then I made another
video and another video

00:05:18.960 --> 00:05:22.715
and another video, and 3600 videos later,

00:05:22.715 --> 00:05:23.912
it's getting better.

00:05:23.912 --> 00:05:26.708
And hopefully, tomorrow's
video is better than today's,

00:05:26.708 --> 00:05:29.921
and tomorrow's Evan is more
impactful than today's,

00:05:29.921 --> 00:05:32.417
and it's an ongoing process of growth.

00:05:32.417 --> 00:05:34.924
The only failure is if I stopped.

00:05:34.924 --> 00:05:36.708
The only failure is if you quit.

00:05:36.708 --> 00:05:38.684
If there's something that
you really want to do

00:05:38.684 --> 00:05:42.259
and you try it the first
time and you suck at it,

00:05:42.259 --> 00:05:43.783
first, expect to suck,

00:05:43.783 --> 00:05:45.988
'cause if it's the first
time you're doing anything,

00:05:45.988 --> 00:05:48.298
you're not going to be
great at it from the start.

00:05:48.298 --> 00:05:51.188
But it's only a real failure
is if you have this dream

00:05:51.188 --> 00:05:53.947
and your first attempt doesn't
work and then you stop.

00:05:53.947 --> 00:05:55.198
That's failure.

00:05:55.198 --> 00:05:57.027
Otherwise, it was just an attempt.

00:05:57.027 --> 00:05:58.935
And tomorrow you try again,
and the next day you try again,

00:05:58.935 --> 00:06:00.833
and every day, you get a little bit better

00:06:00.833 --> 00:06:02.248
and a little bit closer to your goal.

00:06:02.248 --> 00:06:03.638
That's how success works.

00:06:03.638 --> 00:06:04.831
So the question of the day today is,

00:06:04.831 --> 00:06:07.529
I'm curious, how do you define failure?

00:06:07.529 --> 00:06:08.664
What does it mean to you?

00:06:08.664 --> 00:06:09.674
I'm curious.

00:06:09.674 --> 00:06:10.786
Leave it in the comments.

00:06:10.786 --> 00:06:12.227
I'm going to join in the discussion.

00:06:12.227 --> 00:06:14.432
I also want to give a quick
shoutout to Adam Russ.

00:06:14.432 --> 00:06:16.231
Adam, then you so much
for picking up a copy

00:06:16.231 --> 00:06:17.723
of my book, Your One Word.

00:06:17.723 --> 00:06:19.741
It really, really,
really means a lot to me,

00:06:19.741 --> 00:06:21.782
and I hope you enjoy the read.

00:06:21.782 --> 00:06:23.485
So thank you guys again for watching.

00:06:23.485 --> 00:06:24.695
I believe in you.

00:06:24.695 --> 00:06:26.940
I hope you continue to believe in yourself

00:06:26.940 --> 00:06:28.947
and whatever your one word is,

00:06:28.947 --> 00:06:30.537
and I'll see you again tomorrow morning

00:06:30.537 --> 00:06:32.479
for another shot of Entspresso.

00:06:32.479 --> 00:06:35.304
♫ I wake up every morning

00:06:35.304 --> 00:06:39.281
♫ Espresso keep me going

00:06:39.281 --> 00:06:40.114
Hey, Believe Nation.

00:06:40.114 --> 00:06:42.024
If you want a little bit more inspiration

00:06:42.024 --> 00:06:43.743
and motivation around failure,

00:06:43.743 --> 00:06:47.021
here are some bonus clips
that I hope will help.

00:06:47.021 --> 00:06:49.774
After finishing my
adventures on Dark Knight,

00:06:49.774 --> 00:06:51.514
I decided we needed to go

00:06:51.514 --> 00:06:53.513
into a completely different direction.

00:06:53.513 --> 00:06:57.180
(dramatic orchestral music)

00:06:59.697 --> 00:07:02.452
When I think of the notes for the tune,

00:07:02.452 --> 00:07:05.016
I try to think of, "What's the solid world

00:07:05.016 --> 00:07:07.522
"we're going to pour this tune into?"

00:07:07.522 --> 00:07:10.228
And that's what we're doing.

00:07:10.228 --> 00:07:13.935
Basses, can you make your
accents a bit more fierce?

00:07:13.935 --> 00:07:14.969
We're experimenting.

00:07:14.969 --> 00:07:17.969
(dramatic drumming)

00:07:19.711 --> 00:07:20.628
Mighty big.

00:07:22.506 --> 00:07:23.769
That's what you're supposed to do.

00:07:23.769 --> 00:07:26.296
You're not supposed to stand still.

00:07:26.296 --> 00:07:29.586
You don't really want to be observed

00:07:29.586 --> 00:07:31.378
at your greatest failures,

00:07:31.378 --> 00:07:33.308
and you have to invite failure

00:07:33.308 --> 00:07:34.526
if you want to go and experiment,

00:07:34.526 --> 00:07:36.697
and if you're to try to
do something original,

00:07:36.697 --> 00:07:38.467
we have to have a
certain amount of privacy

00:07:38.467 --> 00:07:40.274
to have the courage to fail.

00:07:40.274 --> 00:07:42.985
- I listened to the
people who were successful

00:07:42.985 --> 00:07:44.003
in selling their product.

00:07:44.003 --> 00:07:46.203
I would learn new things
from different ones,

00:07:46.203 --> 00:07:47.950
new approaches, and that's fair.

00:07:47.950 --> 00:07:50.517
You must be a constant student.

00:07:50.517 --> 00:07:53.011
A lot of people are
afraid to try anything new

00:07:53.011 --> 00:07:56.529
because they are afraid that
they will be embarrassed

00:07:56.529 --> 00:07:58.247
until they get good at it.

00:07:58.247 --> 00:08:02.036
Well, one of my favorite sayings is,

00:08:02.036 --> 00:08:04.011
"The most important thing you can do

00:08:04.011 --> 00:08:07.264
"is to understand that
anything worth doing

00:08:07.264 --> 00:08:09.172
"is worth doing poorly."

00:08:09.172 --> 00:08:10.229
- What do you mean by that?

00:08:10.229 --> 00:08:13.700
- Until you can learn to do it well.

00:08:13.700 --> 00:08:16.453
My first few sales calls were pathetic,

00:08:16.453 --> 00:08:19.556
but I knew it was worth
doing, so I kept after it.

00:08:19.556 --> 00:08:22.320
My first few speeches were a disaster,

00:08:22.320 --> 00:08:25.239
but I believed that I
could develop into that,

00:08:25.239 --> 00:08:28.938
so I kept at until I
could do it effectively.

00:08:28.938 --> 00:08:31.461
And all skills are exactly the same way.

00:08:31.461 --> 00:08:33.791
No running back that sets the records

00:08:33.791 --> 00:08:36.987
ever set a record the first
time he picked up a football.

00:08:36.987 --> 00:08:39.220
And you go right down the line with that.

00:08:39.220 --> 00:08:42.422
So if it's worth doing
and you believe in it,

00:08:42.422 --> 00:08:45.014
go ahead and fail, and
the more times you fail,

00:08:45.014 --> 00:08:46.494
the closer you're getting to success

00:08:46.494 --> 00:08:47.790
because you should be learning

00:08:47.790 --> 00:08:50.918
on every simple thing that you do.

00:08:50.918 --> 00:08:53.021
- What have been the best advice

00:08:53.021 --> 00:08:55.757
you received from your
teacher or professor?

00:08:55.757 --> 00:08:57.292
- Ooh, I've gotten a lot of great advice

00:08:57.292 --> 00:08:59.723
from teachers, mentors,
professors, throughout the years.

00:08:59.723 --> 00:09:04.169
So, first of all, you got to
be open to taking some risks

00:09:04.169 --> 00:09:06.469
and not being afraid to fail.

00:09:06.469 --> 00:09:08.568
Failure is the key to success.

00:09:08.568 --> 00:09:11.184
So when you get to college,
you got to raise your hand.

00:09:11.184 --> 00:09:12.262
You have to be engaged.

00:09:12.262 --> 00:09:14.697
Don't be the kid thinking,
"I shouldn't be here,"

00:09:14.697 --> 00:09:17.931
and, "Maybe I'm not smart
enough, and maybe," no.

00:09:17.931 --> 00:09:21.463
You are more than capable
of going to college,

00:09:21.463 --> 00:09:23.518
being successful, and going on to do

00:09:23.518 --> 00:09:24.967
whatever you want in life.

00:09:24.967 --> 00:09:27.000
I sit here because I am you all.

00:09:27.000 --> 00:09:30.494
There is no difference between me and you.

00:09:30.494 --> 00:09:33.969
Working class kid, growing up
on the south side of Chicago,

00:09:33.969 --> 00:09:35.831
growing up with doubts and fears

00:09:35.831 --> 00:09:37.157
just like all of you all do.

00:09:37.157 --> 00:09:39.198
I know exactly how you all are feeling,

00:09:39.198 --> 00:09:41.455
and kids out there who were
thinking, "Maybe I'm not ready.

00:09:41.455 --> 00:09:42.525
"Maybe I'm not good enough.

00:09:42.525 --> 00:09:44.162
"Maybe I can't afford it."

00:09:44.162 --> 00:09:46.196
But what teachers and
mentors have told me is that,

00:09:46.196 --> 00:09:48.674
yes, you can, and you must.

00:09:48.674 --> 00:09:52.924
So get in there and be bold
with your intelligence.

00:09:52.924 --> 00:09:53.994
Raise your hand.

00:09:53.994 --> 00:09:56.271
Get support when you need it.

00:09:56.271 --> 00:10:00.438
And know that we all got here
because somebody helped us.

